Let us now describe more precisely the mechanism that enables one to estimate the discontinuity in the IDS. The following result is well known, but we make it explicit for the reader's benefit. Throughout this discussion, fix a substitution tiling T and associated graph Γ = (V, E). If V ⊆ V is a finite patch, we denote its boundary by ∂V0, which consists of all the tiles in V that share an edge with a tile in V \ V0. A priori, one may be concerned that the degree of the tiles in ∂V are ill-defined. In the specific patches we consider in this paper, one may verify directly that this is not the case.

Definition 2.5. We say P ⊆ V is a good eigenfunction support at energy E if

- (1) P is finite;
- (2) there is a nontrivial eigenfunction ψ of ∆ with ∆Γψ = Eψ and supp(ψ) = P;
- (3) no proper subset of P enjoys the previous property;
- (4) every occurrence of P in V supports an eigenfunction.

Proposition 2.6. Suppose P ⊆ V is a good eigenfunction support at energy E. For any finite patch Γ = (V0, E0), V ⊆ V, the multiplicity of E for ∆ is bounded from below by the largest cardinality of a set of occurrences of P in V with the following properties: no occurrence intersects ∂V and no occurrence is contained in the union of other occurrences.

Proof. Choose a collection of occurrences of P having the enumerated properties. The definitions ensure that each occurrence of P yields an eigenfunction and that the collection of these eigenfunctions is linearly independent.

